Observations

1. The perceptron converged very quickly — within just 2 epochs — because the two clusters of data were well-separated and linearly separable.
2. The learned weights (`w ≈ [0.416, 0.250]`) and bias (`b ≈ -0.100`) produced a single straight-line decision boundary, clearly visible as a diagonal split in the plot.
3. Once trained, the perceptron classified every point in the simulated real-time stream correctly — running accuracy stayed flat at 100% from the very first point onward.
4. The activation values (`w·x + b`) for each point were mostly far from zero, meaning the model was confidently correct rather than making borderline decisions.
5. No point in the stream fell close to the decision boundary, which is consistent with how cleanly separated the training data was.
6. Unlike the feedforward network from Lab 3, the perceptron only outputs a hard 0 or 1 — there's no probability score, so I used the raw net input as a rough stand-in for "confidence."

Conclusions

1. A single perceptron can solve classification problems well, but only when the classes are linearly separable — this experiment worked cleanly because I intentionally used well-separated blob data.
2. The perceptron learning rule is simple but effective: it adjusts weights only when a misclassification occurs, and stops improving once no errors remain (i.e., it converges).
3. This lab reinforced the core limitation of a single-layer perceptron — it cannot model curved or non-linear decision boundaries, which is exactly the gap that motivates moving to a multi-layer feedforward network (as done in Lab 3 with the "moons" dataset).
4. The real-time simulation showed that once a perceptron is trained, classifying new incoming data is computationally trivial and instantaneous — useful for understanding how simple models can still be deployed in real-time systems, as long as the underlying assumption (linear separability) holds.
5. Overall, this lab helped build intuition for *why* deeper networks with non-linear activations (like ReLU) are necessary for anything beyond simple, cleanly separated data.
